# drizzle
# Transient config written by test/repo/migrations-complete.test.ts. It is
# removed in afterAll, but ignored here so a crashed run cannot leave a
# committable orphan in the repo root.
drizzle.config.fresh-clone-test.ts

# Nothing under /drizzle is ignored. drizzle/meta/_journal.json and the
# per-migration snapshots are migration state, not build output: `drizzle-kit
# migrate` reads the journal to decide what to apply, and without it a fresh
# clone silently applies zero migrations and exits 0.
.next-test

# Scratch space for one-off handoffs — never repo content.
#
# `scratch/design-handoff/` holds the design brief exported for attaching to a
# design tool, which cannot read the artifact URL (it renders client-side behind
# a session). The artifact at claude.ai/code/artifact/4c24fa29 remains the
# canonical copy; these are disposable exports of it.
#
# Ignored rather than merely untracked so a `git add -A` cannot sweep them in.
scratch/

# Ad-hoc database probes written at the repo root to query production.
#
# Module resolution forces them here rather than into the scratchpad — `@/`
# aliases and `dotenv` only resolve inside the project — so they sit beside
# tracked files and `git add -A` sweeps them in. That happened: `q-probe.mjs`
# reached commit 1315709, was then deleted, and `dotenv-quiet.test.ts` failed
# reading a tracked file that no longer existed.
#
# Ignored rather than merely remembered, for the reason `scratch/` above gives.
q-probe.mjs
*-probe.mjs

# Design artefacts: working documents, NOT committed — with two exceptions.
#
# SPEC.md is self-contained by design — a link to a file outside it rots while
# the document claims it is still good, and a copy inside the repo is the same
# problem with an extra copy. These are read where they are and left untracked;
# the screenshots that supersede them live in docs/record-detail/.
#
# The exceptions are the wall-and-pull handoff and the TWO build targets it
# indexes — the target split into the wall's (§W, §W.1–§W.37) and the record
# screen's (§1–§10, §12–§27) on 23 Sep. All three move together or not at
# all: the reachability clause is "reachable wherever the index is read, AND
# AT THE SAME REVISION as the index", and a commit carrying one of the three
# is the defect it exists to prevent. Adam's own constraint here — design
# files untracked — is deliberately relaxed for these three, by his call;
# NOTES records why. The target's own reachability note said it seven times: the repo's
# build target ends at §10, so §11 onward "exists only in this file and in an
# excerpt deferring to a section its reader cannot consult", and an excerpt
# naming a source of truth is only sound if the source is reachable. So the
# current target is tracked alongside its handoff, and test/repo/
# handoff-index-complete.test.ts reads a file that CI can see rather than one
# that happened to be on a developer's disk. Re-copied each round; the tracked
# copy is the one the build was measured against.
docs/design/*
!docs/design/HANDOFF-wall-and-pull.md
!docs/design/Record Detail 8a - build target.dc.html
# §1-§10, split out of the live target and closed. Tracked with it: the handoff's
# reachability rule is that a source of truth is reachable wherever the index is
# read, AND at the same revision as the index.
!docs/design/Record Detail 8a - settled 1-10.dc.html
!docs/design/Wall and Pull - build target.dc.html

# The same artefacts' STALE root copies, from before they moved into
# docs/design/. Each root file is smaller than its docs/design counterpart, so
# the moved ones are current. Ignored rather than deleted: they are Adam's
# working files and a diff I cannot prove is a duplicate is not mine to remove.
/*.dc.html
